980 0 obj
<<
/Title (Compress model updates before sending them, Eg: by using fewer bits to represent values. This reduces the amount of data transferred.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 982 0 R
/First 983 0 R
/Last 983 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
981 0 obj
<<
/Title (Each intersection trains its model locally and sends updates to the server after every communication round. Frequent communication can increase the total data exchanged between clients and the server.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 979 0 R
/Next 984 0 R
>>
endobj
982 0 obj
<<
/Title (Each intersection sends full size model updates to the server, which can use a lot of communication bandwidth.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 985 0 R
/Next 980 0 R
>>
endobj
983 0 obj
<<
/Title (A3-PS003-TC122)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 980 0 R
>>
endobj
984 0 obj
<<
/Title (Allow each intersection to train for more local epochs before sending its updates. This can reduce communication rounds and save network costs while maintaining good prediction accuracy.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 981 0 R
/Next 986 0 R
>>
endobj
985 0 obj
<<
/Title (3. Model update compression)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 987 0 R
/Next 982 0 R
>>
endobj
986 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Better learning with different traffic patterns \(FedProx\))
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 984 0 R
/Next 988 0 R
>>
endobj
987 0 obj
<<
/Title (Use FedProx, which helps keep each client's model closer to the global model during local training. This may improve learning when intersections have different traffic patterns.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 988 0 R
/Next 985 0 R
>>
endobj
988 0 obj
<<
/Title (Different intersections may have different traffic volumes, rush hours, and congestion levels. Standard FedAvg may struggle to learn well when these local data patterns are very different \(Non-IID data\).)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 972 0 R
/Prev 986 0 R
/Next 987 0 R
>>
endobj
